---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Trust & safety"
  description="How OriginsLynk handles access, credentials, isolation, monitoring, backups, and offboarding — and what we deliberately do not claim."
>
  <section class="section-cream page-hero">
    <div class="container container-narrow">
      <p class="eyebrow">Trust &amp; safety</p>
      <h1>How access actually works.</h1>
      <p class="lede" style="margin-top: var(--space-5)">
        Automation is only worth having if you can describe, out loud, what it is
        permitted to do. This page is that description. It is written to be checked, not
        to be reassuring.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-sunken principle-section">
    <div class="container container-narrow">
      <div class="principle">
        <h2>The envelope</h2>
        <p class="lede" style="margin-top: var(--space-4)">
          Read the inputs. Interpret them. Prepare the work. Leave it as a draft or a
          file. A person decides what happens next.
        </p>
        <p class="muted" style="margin-top: var(--space-4)">
          Nothing we build sends an email, publishes a post, moves money, or deletes a
          record on its own. If a workflow would need to, that is a separate conversation
          with its own design and your explicit written approval — never a setting we
          quietly enable.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="container">
      <div class="pillars">
        <article>
          <h3>Human approval where it matters</h3>
          <p>
            Every workflow has a defined point where output waits for a person. You will
            know where that point is, because it is written into the workflow's
            specification before it is built.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article>
          <h3>Least access that works</h3>
          <p>
            Each connection gets the narrowest permission that does the job. If a
            workflow only needs to read your inbox and create drafts, that is all it is
            authorized to do — not full account access because it was simpler to set up.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article>
          <h3>Isolated deployments</h3>
          <p>
            Each managed client receives a dedicated environment with separate credentials,
            settings, and configuration backups. This separation reduces the risk of one
            client's system affecting another.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article id="data-handling">
          <h3>Credentials are handled separately</h3>
          <p>
            Passwords and access codes are not collected through the public contact form or
            ordinary email. Account access is entered directly or shared through an approved
            secure method after the scope is agreed.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article>
          <h3>Monitored, with alerts pointed at us</h3>
          <p>
            Health checks, failure alerts, and a dead-man's switch on the backup job. The
            alerts reach us first. You should hear about a problem from a person, not
            discover it from silence.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article>
          <h3>Configuration backups are checked</h3>
          <p>
            The managed configuration and workflow state described in your agreement are
            backed up and checked. These backups do not replace the backup responsibilities
            of every outside account connected to the system.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article>
          <h3>Revocation is yours, not ours</h3>
          <p>
            Access is granted from your own accounts, which means you can withdraw it from
            your own accounts, immediately, without asking us and without waiting for a
            business day.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article>
          <h3>Export and offboarding on request</h3>
          <p>
            When service ends, OriginsLynk provides the available configuration, removes
            managed access and credentials, and schedules deletion according to the service
            agreement. Rented hardware is then returned.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ THE HONEST PART ============ -->
  <section class="section-dark disclosure-section">
    <div class="container container-narrow">
      <p class="eyebrow">The part most vendors leave out</p>
      <h2>What we do not claim.</h2>

      <div class="disclosure">
        <h3>We hold credentials for managed clients</h3>
        <p>
          Managed automation runs without you, which means the connection to your accounts
          is stored on a dedicated appliance we administer. That access is limited to the
          agreed work, documented in the service agreement, and revocable from the accounts
          you own.
        </p>
        <p class="small muted" style="margin-top: var(--space-3)">
          If you are not comfortable with that — and it is reasonable not to be — the
          Personal AI Assistant runs on your own machine and never has this property.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="disclosure">
        <h3>We hold no compliance certifications</h3>
        <p>
          We are not SOC 2 certified, not HIPAA compliant, and not ISO 27001 audited. We
          are a small operation that follows sound practice, and we would rather say that
          plainly than imply a certification we do not have. If your work requires one,
          you need a vendor who has been audited for it.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="disclosure">
        <h3>AI gets things wrong</h3>
        <p>
          The models we configure will occasionally misread context, state something
          confidently that is incorrect, or produce a draft that misses the point. This is
          exactly why the approval step exists and why nothing sends itself. Part of what
          we train you on is recognizing when output is wrong — including how to check a
          claim before you act on it.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="disclosure">
        <h3>Sensitive and regulated work is excluded by default</h3>
        <p>
          Workflows touching regulated data or decisions with legal consequence are out of
          scope unless separately assessed and designed. We would rather turn down that
          work than build it casually.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="container container-narrow cta">
      <h2>Questions about any of this?</h2>
      <p class="lede" style="margin-top: var(--space-4)">
        Ask them before you buy something, not after. If something here does not fit how
        you need to work, that is worth finding out in the first conversation.
      </p>
      <div class="btn-row">
        <a href="/contact" class="btn btn-primary">Request a workflow review</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .principle {
    padding: var(--space-6);
    background: var(--ground-raised);
    border: var(--border-thin) solid var(--rule);
    border-left: var(--border-thick) solid var(--accent);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
  }

  .principle h2 { font-size: var(--text-2xl); }

  .page-hero h1 { max-width: 15ch; }
  .page-hero .lede { max-width: 61ch; }

  .pillars {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-6) var(--space-7);
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 18rem), 1fr));
  }

  .pillars h3 {
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-3);
    padding-top: var(--space-3);
    border-top: var(--border-thin) solid var(--rule-strong);
  }

  .pillars p { font-size: var(--text-sm); color: var(--ink-muted); }

  .disclosure {
    margin-top: var(--space-6);
    padding-top: var(--space-5);
    border-top: var(--border-thin) solid var(--rule-strong);
  }

  .disclosure h3 { font-size: var(--text-lg); margin-bottom: var(--space-3); }
  .disclosure p { color: var(--ink-muted); }
  .disclosure-section .disclosure { border-color: var(--rule-inverse); }
  .disclosure-section .disclosure p { color: var(--ink-inverse-muted); }
  .disclosure-section .disclosure h3 { color: var(--ink-inverse); }
  .disclosure-section .disclosure .small { color: #aab3ad; }

  .cta { text-align: center; }
  .cta p { margin-inline: auto; }
  .cta .btn-row { justify-content: center; }
</style>
